1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,000
Cancer Immunotherapy: How the Body Fights Cancer.

2
00:00:03,950 --> 00:00:13,650
Cancer immunotherapy represents one of the most transformative advances in oncology, harnessing the body's own immune system to recognize and destroy malignant cells.

3
00:00:13,650 --> 00:00:23,537
A 2025 review in Nature Medicine described immunotherapy as the fourth pillar of cancer treatment, alongside surgery, chemotherapy, and radiation.

4
00:00:23,537 --> 00:00:29,287
The approach has fundamentally changed prognosis for several previously intractable cancers.

5
00:00:29,287 --> 00:00:33,400
The cornerstone of immunotherapy is immune checkpoint blockade.

6
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:39,875
Cancer cells exploit checkpoint proteins such as PD-L1 to trick T-cells into inactivity.

7
00:00:39,875 --> 00:00:46,000
Drugs called checkpoint inhibitors block this interaction, liberating T-cells to attack tumors.

8
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:56,100
Approved by the FDA starting in 2011, these agents have shown remarkable efficacy in melanoma, non-small cell lung cancer, and renal cell carcinoma.

9
00:00:56,100 --> 00:01:06,650
A NEJM-published trial of the checkpoint inhibitor pembrolizumab in advanced melanoma reported five-year survival rates of 34 percent, compared to less than 10 percent historically.

10
00:01:06,650 --> 00:01:12,475
Chimeric antigen receptor (CAR) T-cell therapy represents another breakthrough.

11
00:01:12,475 --> 00:01:22,637
This approach involves extracting a patient's T-cells, genetically engineering them to express receptors that recognize cancer-specific proteins, and reinfusing them.

12
00:01:22,637 --> 00:01:29,262
In certain B-cell malignancies, CAR-T therapy produces complete remission rates exceeding 80 percent.

13
00:01:29,262 --> 00:01:34,075
The FDA has approved six CAR-T products since 2017.

14
00:01:34,075 --> 00:01:45,075
A study in Cell demonstrated that next-generation CAR-T cells engineered with logic-gated circuits can distinguish cancer cells from healthy tissue more precisely, reducing side effects.

15
00:01:45,075 --> 00:01:48,487
The latest frontier is cancer vaccination.

16
00:01:48,487 --> 00:01:55,400
Unlike preventive vaccines, therapeutic cancer vaccines train the immune system to attack existing tumors.

17
00:01:55,400 --> 00:02:05,712
The NIH National Cancer Institute is sponsoring multiple phase 3 trials of personalized neoantigen vaccines, which target mutations unique to each patient's cancer.

18
00:02:05,712 --> 00:02:13,075
Combined with checkpoint inhibitors, these vaccines have shown 50 percent reduction in disease progression in early trials.

19
00:02:13,075 --> 00:02:22,725
The evidence is clear: the immune system can be taught to fight cancer, and immunotherapy is rapidly becoming the standard of care for an expanding list of malignancies.

